      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.639,
            0.049,
            0.846,
            0.066
          ],
          "content": "Constructions and Tilings"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.088,
            0.847,
            0.128
          ],
          "content": "This method of constructing the perpendicular bisector is not only geometrically exact but also a practical way to construct it accurately."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.127,
            0.847,
            0.167
          ],
          "content": "This method to find the midpoint of a line segment is more accurate than measuring the length using a marked scale."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.184,
            0.678,
            0.205
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Construction of a 90° Angle at a Given Point"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.209,
            0.53,
            0.308
          ],
          "content": "Can we extend the method of constructing the perpendicular bisector to construct a 90° angle at any point on a line? Draw a line and mark a point O on it. Construct a 90° angle at point O."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.315,
            0.528,
            0.356
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Find a segment of this line for which O is the midpoint."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.175,
            0.36,
            0.515,
            0.381
          ],
          "content": "Extend the line on either side of O."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.386,
            0.529,
            0.443
          ],
          "content": "Using a compass, mark two points X and Y at equal distance from O, so that O is the midpoint of XY."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.443,
            0.528,
            0.5
          ],
          "content": "The perpendicular bisector of XY will pass through O and is perpendicular to the given line."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.501,
            0.526,
            0.558
          ],
          "content": "In this case, do we need to draw two pairs of intersecting arcs to get the perpendicular bisector of XY?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.558,
            0.529,
            0.614
          ],
          "content": "No, we don’t. We already have one point, O, lying on the perpendicular bisector."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.615,
            0.527,
            0.674
          ],
          "content": "Figures 6.2 and 6.3 describe the steps to construct a 90° angle at a given point on a line."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.539,
            0.216,
            0.884,
            0.661
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_5_image_12.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.691,
            0.612,
            0.712
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Construction Methods in Śulba-Sūtras"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.718,
            0.847,
            0.775
          ],
          "content": "Ancient mathematicians from different civilizations, including India, knew exact procedures to construct perpendiculars and perpendicular bisectors."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.775,
            0.849,
            0.893
          ],
          "content": "In India, the earliest known texts containing these methods are the Śulba-Sūtras. These are geometric texts of Vedic period dealing with the construction of fire altars for rituals. The Śulba-Sūtras are part of one of the six Vedāṅgas (a term that literally means ‘limbs of the Vedas’). The Śulbas contain the methods that we developed earlier to construct a perpendicular and the perpendicular bisector. All the construction"
        },
        {
